::ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.7] Which function shows exponential growth? {
=A. \(y=40(1.25)^x\) #Correct. The base 1.25 is greater than 1, so the model grows.
~B. \(y=40(0.75)^x\) #A base between 0 and 1 shows decay.
~C. \(y=40+1.25x\) #This is linear because x is multiplied, not used as an exponent.
~D. \(y=40x^2\) #This is quadratic, not exponential.
}

::ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.7] A value starts at 500 and decreases by 12% each year. Which model represents the situation? {
~A. \(y=500(1.12)^x\) #This shows 12% growth, not decay.
=B. \(y=500(0.88)^x\) #Correct. A 12% decrease leaves 88%, so the decay factor is 0.88.
~C. \(y=500-12x\) #This subtracts a fixed amount, not a percent of the current amount.
~D. \(y=12(500)^x\) #This reverses the initial value and base.
}

::ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.8] What is the initial value of \(y=18(2)^x\)? {
~A. 2 #The base is 2, which is the multiplier, not the initial value.
~B. 20 #This adds the coefficient and base, which is not how initial value is found.
=C. 18 #Correct. When \(x=0\), \(2^0=1\), so \(y=18\).
~D. 36 #This is the value after one multiplication by 2, not the initial value.
}

::ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.9] A bacteria culture doubles every hour from an initial amount of 75. What does the 2 represent in \(B(t)=75(2)^t\)? {
~A. The starting amount #The starting amount is 75.
~B. The number of hours already passed #The exponent t represents time in hours.
~C. The final amount #The final amount depends on the value of t.
=D. The growth factor each hour #Correct. Multiplying by 2 each hour means the culture doubles.
}

::ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L06_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.8] A table has outputs 6, 18, 54, 162 as x increases by 1. What is the common multiplier? {
~A. 6 #The first output is not the multiplier.
=B. 3 #Correct. Each output is multiplied by 3 to get the next output.
~C. 12 #This is the first difference, but exponential models use a common multiplier.
~D. 18 #This is one output value, not the ratio between consecutive outputs.
}
